            <ArticleTitle>[The elderly as heart surgery patients. Pre- and postoperative analysis of the quality of life].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>547-51</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="UNLABELLED">The aim of the study was to measure pre- and postoperative quality of life of the old patient to find out his profit of open heart surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">71 patients ranging from 75 to 86 years (mean 77.6 years) were examined by using a standard interview about their quality of life on the day of hospital admittance and one year after returning home (range 11-14 months).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There was an improvement of postoperative quality of life. These results were similar to data reported in the literature after investigating younger patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">No one should be excluded from open heart surgery because of old age.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
